To configure the Figma MCP server, you will typically add a configuration block to your `mcp.json` file (often located in your IDE's MCP configuration directory)[1][2].

The configuration format depends on whether you are using the **Remote** (recommended) or **Desktop** version of the server[1][3].

### 1. Remote Figma MCP Server (Recommended)
This version connects directly to Figma without requiring the desktop app[2]. Add the following to your `mcp.json`[1]:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "figma": {
      "url": "https://mcp.figma.com/mcp",
      "type": "http"
    }
  }
}
```
*(Note: Some clients use an `"servers"` key instead of `"mcpServers"` depending on the specific MCP implementation being used[1][3].)*

---

### 2. Desktop Figma MCP Server
The desktop version runs locally via the Figma desktop app[3]. First, ensure you have enabled the **Desktop MCP Server** in Figma (found in the Inspect panel under Dev Mode)[3][4].

It uses the local URL `http://127.0.0.1:3845/mcp`[3][5]. Your `mcp.json` configuration should look like this[3]:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "figma-desktop": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:3845/mcp"
    }
  }
}
```

---

### 3. Community/Third-Party Server (`figma-developer-mcp`)
If you are using the community-maintained `figma-developer-mcp` (often used for Cursor or similar agents), it requires a Figma API key[6][7]. The configuration looks like this[7]:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "figma-developer-mcp": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "figma-developer-mcp", "--stdio"],
      "env": {
        "FIGMA_API_KEY": "YOUR_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

